!deck: Math Identities
!tags: Math Trigonometry Inverse-Function
!type: Basic Math

!id: kE1aYt6R2g
!front: What are the principal values of an inverse trigonometric function?
!back:
- The output angles selected by restricting the original trigonometric function to a **one-to-one** branch.
- The **range** of the inverse function.
~~~

!type: Cloze Math

!id: uh5XSfdD7B
!text: The domain of \( \arcsin \) is {{c1::\( [-1,1] \)}}, and its range is {{c1::\( [-\pi/2,\pi/2] \)}}.
!back extra:
![Sine restricted to theta from minus pi over two to pi over two and its reflection across y equals x, an increasing arcsine curve from minus one minus pi over two to one pi over two](img/graph-arcsin.svg)

The principal values \( [-\pi/2,\pi/2] \) trace the closed right semicircle.

![Right semicircle emphasized from minus pi over two to pi over two with both endpoints filled, showing every possible principal value of arcsin on the unit circle](img/range-arcsin.svg)
~~~

!id: 2898u48kql
!text: The domain of \( \arccos \) is {{c1::\( [-1,1] \)}}, and its range is {{c1::\( [0,\pi] \)}}.
!back extra:
![Cosine restricted to zero through pi and its reflection across y equals x, a decreasing arccosine curve from minus one pi to one zero](img/graph-arccos.svg)

The principal values \( [0,\pi] \) trace the closed upper semicircle.

![Upper semicircle emphasized from zero to pi with both endpoints filled, showing every possible principal value of arccos on the unit circle](img/range-arccos.svg)
~~~

!id: BztlAhmRUq
!text: The domain of \( \arctan \) is {{c1::\( \mathbb R \)}}, and its range is {{c1::\( (-\pi/2,\pi/2) \)}}.
!back extra:
![Central tangent branch and its reflection across y equals x, an increasing arctangent curve through the origin approaching plus and minus pi over two](img/graph-arctan.svg)

The principal values \( (-\pi/2,\pi/2) \) trace the open right semicircle.

![Right semicircle emphasized from minus pi over two to pi over two with both endpoints open, showing the principal values of arctan](img/range-arctan.svg)
~~~

!id: Xaz9X4XgQ3
!text: The domain of \( \operatorname{arccot} \) is {{c1::\( \mathbb R \)}}, and its range is {{c1::\( (0,\pi) \)}}.
!back extra:
![Cotangent restricted to zero through pi and its reflection across y equals x, a decreasing arccotangent curve approaching pi and zero](img/graph-arccot.svg)

The principal values \( (0,\pi) \) trace the open upper semicircle.

![Upper semicircle emphasized from zero to pi with both endpoints open, showing the principal values of arccot under the zero-to-pi convention](img/range-arccot.svg)
~~~

!id: ONDmcq3ktC
!text: The domain of \( \operatorname{arcsec} \) is {{c1::\( (-\infty,-1]\cup[1,\infty) \)}}, and its range is {{c1::\( [0,\pi/2)\cup(\pi/2,\pi] \)}}.
!back extra:
![Secant restricted to zero through pi except pi over two and its reflection across y equals x, two arcsecant branches approaching pi over two](img/graph-arcsec.svg)

The principal values \( [0,\pi/2)\cup(\pi/2,\pi] \) trace the upper semicircle with \( \pi/2 \) removed.

![Upper semicircle emphasized with filled endpoints at zero and pi and an open point at pi over two, showing the principal values of arcsec](img/range-arcsec.svg)
~~~

!id: 7UpMnYLY82
!text: The domain of \( \operatorname{arccsc} \) is {{c1::\( (-\infty,-1]\cup[1,\infty) \)}}, and its range is {{c1::\( [-\pi/2,0)\cup(0,\pi/2] \)}}.
!back extra:
![Cosecant restricted to minus pi over two through zero and zero through pi over two and its reflection across y equals x, two arccosecant branches approaching zero](img/graph-arccsc.svg)

The principal values \( [-\pi/2,0)\cup(0,\pi/2] \) trace the right semicircle with \( 0 \) removed.

![Right semicircle emphasized with filled endpoints at minus pi over two and pi over two and an open point at zero, showing the principal values of arccsc](img/range-arccsc.svg)
~~~

!type: Cloze Math

!id: WIpisDTzy0
!text: The principal-value arc of \( \arcsin \) is its range \( {{c1::[-\pi/2,\pi/2]}} \) marked as angles on the unit circle, it becomes {{c1::the closed right semicircle::semicircle}}.
!back extra:
![Right semicircle emphasized from minus pi over two to pi over two with both endpoints filled, showing every possible principal value of arcsin on the unit circle](img/range-arcsin.svg)

Sweeping \( \theta \) from \( -\pi/2 \) (bottom) through \( 0 \) to \( \pi/2 \) (top) covers exactly the right half. Both endpoints are included because the range is closed.

So for \( \theta=\arcsin x \), \( \cos\theta\ge0 \), while \( \sin\theta=x \) has the sign of \( x \).
~~~

!id: no83IqQSSl
!text: The principal-value arc of \( \arccos \) is its range \( {{c1::[0,\pi]}} \) marked as angles on the unit circle, it becomes {{c1::the closed upper semicircle::semicircle}}.
!back extra:
![Upper semicircle emphasized from zero to pi with both endpoints filled, showing every possible principal value of arccos on the unit circle](img/range-arccos.svg)

Sweeping \( \theta \) from \( 0 \) (right) through \( \pi/2 \) to \( \pi \) (left) covers exactly the upper half. Both endpoints are included because the range is closed.

So for \( \theta=\arccos x \), \( \sin\theta\ge0 \), while \( \cos\theta=x \) has the sign of \( x \).
~~~

!id: o2X97SKHOl
!text: The principal-value arc of \( \arctan \) is its range \( {{c1::(-\pi/2,\pi/2)}} \) marked as angles on the unit circle, it becomes {{c1::the open right semicircle::semicircle}}.
!back extra:
![Right semicircle emphasized from minus pi over two to pi over two with both endpoints open, showing the principal values of arctan](img/range-arctan.svg)

Sweeping \( \theta \) from \( -\pi/2 \) (bottom) through \( 0 \) to \( \pi/2 \) (top) covers exactly the right half. Both endpoints are excluded because the range is open, since tangent is undefined at \( \pm\pi/2 \).

So for \( \theta=\arctan x \), \( \cos\theta>0 \), while \( \sin\theta \) has the sign of \( x=\tan\theta \).
~~~

!id: SOwgxj9eQf
!text: The principal-value arc of \( \operatorname{arccot} \) is its range \( {{c1::(0,\pi)}} \) marked as angles on the unit circle, it becomes {{c1::the open upper semicircle::semicircle}}.
!back extra:
![Upper semicircle emphasized from zero to pi with both endpoints open, showing the principal values of arccot under the zero-to-pi convention](img/range-arccot.svg)

Sweeping \( \theta \) from \( 0 \) (right) through \( \pi/2 \) to \( \pi \) (left) covers exactly the upper half. Both endpoints are excluded because the range is open, since cotangent is undefined at \( 0 \) and \( \pi \).

So for \( \theta=\operatorname{arccot}x \), \( \sin\theta>0 \), while \( \cos\theta \) has the sign of \( x=\cot\theta \).
~~~

!id: ptjIK2M45D
!text: The principal-value arc of \( \operatorname{arcsec} \) is its range \( {{c1::[0,\pi/2)\cup(\pi/2,\pi]}} \) marked as angles on the unit circle, it becomes {{c1::the upper semicircle with \( 0 \) and \( \pi \) included but \( \pi/2 \) excluded::semicircle}}.
!back extra:
![Upper semicircle emphasized with filled endpoints at zero and pi and an open point at pi over two, showing the principal values of arcsec](img/range-arcsec.svg)

Sweeping \( \theta \) from \( 0 \) (right) to \( \pi \) (left) covers the upper half. The endpoints \( 0 \) and \( \pi \) are included, but the top point \( \pi/2 \) is removed from the range because secant is undefined there.

So for \( \theta=\operatorname{arcsec}x \), \( \sin\theta\ge0 \), while \( \cos\theta=1/x \) has the sign of \( x \).
~~~

!id: ijBjm2lUx8
!text: The principal-value arc of \( \operatorname{arccsc} \) is its range \( {{c1::[-\pi/2,0)\cup(0,\pi/2]}} \) marked as angles on the unit circle, it becomes {{c1::the right semicircle with \( \pm\pi/2 \) included but \( 0 \) excluded::semicircle}}.
!back extra:
![Right semicircle emphasized with filled endpoints at minus pi over two and pi over two and an open point at zero, showing the principal values of arccsc](img/range-arccsc.svg)

Sweeping \( \theta \) from \( -\pi/2 \) (bottom) to \( \pi/2 \) (top) covers the right half. The endpoints \( \pm\pi/2 \) are included, but the rightmost point \( 0 \) is removed from the range because cosecant is undefined there.

So for \( \theta=\operatorname{arccsc}x \), \( \cos\theta\ge0 \), while \( \sin\theta=1/x \) has the sign of \( x \).
~~~

!type: Basic Math

!id: BnwFjLmBaG
!front: How does the graph of \( y=\arcsin x \) relate to the graph of sine?
!back:
![Sine restricted to theta from minus pi over two to pi over two and its reflection across the diagonal y equals x, an increasing inverse curve from minus one minus pi over two to one pi over two](img/graph-arcsin.svg)

Restrict sine to \( [-\pi/2,\pi/2] \), then reflect that branch across \( y=x \). The inverse rises from \( (-1,-\pi/2) \) through \( (0,0) \) to \( (1,\pi/2) \).
~~~

!id: 62mNRMpWZs
!front:
Which inverse trig function has this graph?

![An increasing inverse curve from x minus one at height minus pi over two through the origin to x one at height pi over two](img/graph-arcsin-quiz.svg)
!back: \[ y=\arcsin x \]
~~~

!id: HFEBqBu3kB
!front: How does the graph of \( y=\arccos x \) relate to the graph of cosine?
!back:
![Cosine restricted to zero through pi and its reflection across y equals x, a decreasing inverse curve from minus one pi to one zero](img/graph-arccos.svg)

Restrict cosine to \( [0,\pi] \), then reflect that branch across \( y=x \). The inverse decreases from \( (-1,\pi) \) through \( (0,\pi/2) \) to \( (1,0) \).
~~~

!id: l6vUy12nHk
!front:
Which inverse trig function has this graph?

![A decreasing inverse curve from x minus one at height pi through x zero at height pi over two to x one at height zero](img/graph-arccos-quiz.svg)
!back: \[ y=\arccos x \]
~~~

!id: 7Ui0di94hQ
!front: How does the graph of \( y=\arctan x \) relate to the graph of tangent?
!back:
![Central increasing tangent branch and its reflection across y equals x, an increasing inverse curve through the origin approaching horizontal lines at plus and minus pi over two](img/graph-arctan.svg)

Restrict tangent to \( (-\pi/2,\pi/2) \), then reflect across \( y=x \). The inverse increases through \( (0,0) \) and approaches the horizontal asymptotes \( y=\pm\pi/2 \).
~~~

!id: LhetGuZPUt
!front:
Which inverse trig function has this graph?

![An increasing curve through the origin, approaching horizontal asymptotes at plus and minus pi over two](img/graph-arctan-quiz.svg)
!back: \[ y=\arctan x \]
~~~

!id: 2dvpVXAn5e
!front: How does the graph of \( y=\operatorname{arccot}x \) relate to the graph of cotangent?
!back:
![Cotangent restricted to zero through pi and its reflection across y equals x, a decreasing inverse curve through zero pi over two approaching heights pi and zero](img/graph-arccot.svg)

Restrict cotangent to \( (0,\pi) \), then reflect across \( y=x \). The inverse decreases through \( (0,\pi/2) \), approaching \( y=\pi \) as \( x\to-\infty \) and \( y=0 \) as \( x\to+\infty \).
~~~

!id: k1K72a3TKK
!front:
Which inverse trig function with range \( (0,\pi) \) has this graph?

![A decreasing curve through x zero at height pi over two, approaching height pi on the left and zero on the right](img/graph-arccot-quiz.svg)
!back: \[ y=\operatorname{arccot}x \]
~~~

!id: J8ETycE2dF
!front: How does the graph of \( y=\operatorname{arcsec}x \) relate to the graph of secant?
!back:
![Secant restricted to zero through pi except pi over two and its reflection across y equals x, two inverse branches beginning at one zero and minus one pi and approaching pi over two](img/graph-arcsec.svg)

Reflect secant restricted to that range across \( y=x \). The inverse has two branches: \( (1,0) \) approaches \( y=\pi/2 \) from below as \( x\to+\infty \); \( (-1,\pi) \) approaches it from above as \( x\to-\infty \).
~~~

!id: HIF6yfZV6Y
!front:
Which inverse trig function with range \( [0,\pi/2)\cup(\pi/2,\pi] \) has this graph?

![Two branches defined for x at least one or at most minus one, passing through one zero and minus one pi respectively and approaching height pi over two from opposite sides](img/graph-arcsec-quiz.svg)
!back: \[ y=\operatorname{arcsec}x \]
~~~

!id: TIHk5LlWQo
!front: How does the graph of \( y=\operatorname{arccsc}x \) relate to the graph of cosecant?
!back:
![Cosecant restricted to minus pi over two through zero and zero through pi over two and its reflection across y equals x, two inverse branches approaching height zero](img/graph-arccsc.svg)

Reflect cosecant restricted to that range across \( y=x \). The inverse has branches through \( (1,\pi/2) \) and \( (-1,-\pi/2) \), both approaching the horizontal asymptote \( y=0 \) as \( |x|\to\infty \).
~~~

!id: HmyZcey1ES
!front:
Which inverse trig function with range \( [-\pi/2,0)\cup(0,\pi/2] \) has this graph?

![Two branches defined for x at least one or at most minus one, passing through one pi over two and minus one minus pi over two and approaching height zero from above and below](img/graph-arccsc-quiz.svg)
!back: \[ y=\operatorname{arccsc}x \]
~~~
